WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:03.702
Good morning, listeners.

2
00:00:03.652 --> 00:00:07.476
Today is September 22, 2026.

3
00:00:07.476 --> 00:00:14.657
Overnight, the broad tape is telling a story of election-driven positioning that has dominated the last six weeks of trading.

4
00:00:14.657 --> 00:00:21.300
The S and P five hundred is grinding through what UBS models as the worst volatility window of the year.

5
00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:26.373
The Nasdaq and the Dow are both showing the classic pre-midterm compression pattern.

6
00:00:26.373 --> 00:00:28.910
Treasury yields are elevated.

7
00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:33.849
The market is pricing a Fed that cannot cut aggressively while oil stays pinned.

8
00:00:33.849 --> 00:00:38.064
The rate path is the single biggest macro variable right now.

9
00:00:38.064 --> 00:00:40.282
The consensus is clear.

10
00:00:40.282 --> 00:00:43.836
September through early October is the danger zone.

11
00:00:43.836 --> 00:00:51.569
Across nineteen complete midterm cycles, the S and P returns a median of minus one point four percent in this window.

12
00:00:51.569 --> 00:00:54.547
Volatility is at its annual peak.

13
00:00:54.547 --> 00:00:57.942
Funds are de-risking and locking in gains.

14
00:00:57.942 --> 00:01:01.888
Any geopolitical headline gets amplified tenfold.

15
00:01:01.888 --> 00:01:04.768
But here is the divergence that matters.

16
00:01:04.768 --> 00:01:08.603
The primary bull case is the post-election snapback.

17
00:01:08.603 --> 00:01:14.363
Nineteen out of nineteen midterm cycles have produced a positive twelve-month return after the vote.

18
00:01:14.363 --> 00:01:17.268
The median is plus fourteen percent.

19
00:01:17.268 --> 00:01:20.699
UBS models three months at plus six.

20
00:01:20.699 --> 00:01:23.113
Six months at plus ten.

21
00:01:23.113 --> 00:01:26.275
Twelve months at plus fourteen and a half.

22
00:01:26.275 --> 00:01:33.959
The only three exceptions in a century were one thousand nine hundred seventy-four, two thousand two, and two thousand eighteen.

23
00:01:33.959 --> 00:01:38.162
All three overlapped with recession and aggressive Fed tightening.

24
00:01:38.162 --> 00:01:41.189
The key question is which Congress you get.

25
00:01:41.189 --> 00:01:46.826
Bank of America models put the split Congress scenario at forty-three percent probability.

26
00:01:46.826 --> 00:01:50.883
Democrats taking both chambers sits at forty-four percent.

27
00:01:50.883 --> 00:01:54.572
Republicans holding both is the low-probability tail.

28
00:01:54.572 --> 00:01:57.635
And the market preference is unambiguous.

29
00:01:57.635 --> 00:02:01.826
A split Congress is the single best scenario for equities.

30
00:02:01.826 --> 00:02:05.307
Policy paralysis means no tax hikes.

31
00:02:05.307 --> 00:02:07.905
No antitrust enforcement.

32
00:02:07.905 --> 00:02:10.368
No energy restrictions.

33
00:02:10.368 --> 00:02:17.170
The BofA split Congress basket has outperformed the S and P by seventeen percent over recent cycles.

34
00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:23.530
The constituents are Micron, Intel, Exxon Mobil, Goldman Sachs, Lockheed Martin.

35
00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:27.402
Chips, energy, financials, defense.

36
00:02:27.402 --> 00:02:29.204
All protected.

37
00:02:29.204 --> 00:02:31.704
No policy black swan.

38
00:02:31.704 --> 00:02:34.621
The bear case is the Democratic sweep.

39
00:02:34.621 --> 00:02:38.015
And this is where the thesis collision gets violent.

40
00:02:38.015 --> 00:02:42.145
BofA warns of a ten percent drawdown in the S and P.

41
00:02:42.145 --> 00:02:45.209
The AI sector faces a double hit.

42
00:02:45.209 --> 00:02:49.817
Corporate tax increases plus aggressive tech antitrust enforcement.

43
00:02:49.817 --> 00:02:55.969
The current AI bull market pricing has not fully priced in a tech tax and a regulatory crackdown.

44
00:02:55.969 --> 00:02:58.800
That is a massive re-rating risk.

45
00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.912
Now let's talk tech and capital flows.

46
00:03:01.912 --> 00:03:05.773
The AI capex cycle is at its inflection point.

47
00:03:05.773 --> 00:03:11.079
Hyperscaler spending on data centers and GPU clusters is still expanding.

48
00:03:11.079 --> 00:03:15.932
But the marginal dollar is getting harder to justify at current valuations.

49
00:03:15.932 --> 00:03:20.025
The semiconductor supply chain is the transmission mechanism.

50
00:03:20.025 --> 00:03:24.645
Micron and Intel sit in that sweet spot of the split Congress basket.

51
00:03:24.645 --> 00:03:29.510
They benefit from continued chip subsidies without the regulatory overhang.

52
00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:35.270
But if Democrats take the House, the antitrust file on the chip sector gets reopened.

53
00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:39.228
That is a direct hit to the entire supply chain narrative.

54
00:03:39.228 --> 00:03:43.089
Oil is the hidden variable nobody is pricing correctly.

55
00:03:43.089 --> 00:03:47.880
Chevron's CEO has publicly pushed back on the White House narrative.

56
00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:51.790
He says there is no path to rapid price normalization.

57
00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:54.547
Strategic reserves are depleted.

58
00:03:54.547 --> 00:03:57.488
Inventories are at multi-year lows.

59
00:03:57.488 --> 00:04:01.287
The Saudi supply disruption has tightened the market further.

60
00:04:01.287 --> 00:04:06.447
If the Middle East conflict keeps oil elevated, inflation stays sticky.

61
00:04:06.447 --> 00:04:08.567
The Fed cannot cut.

62
00:04:08.567 --> 00:04:10.675
Growth stocks bleed.

63
00:04:10.675 --> 00:04:14.094
This is true regardless of who wins Congress.

64
00:04:14.094 --> 00:04:16.826
Let's go deep on three names.

65
00:04:16.928 --> 00:04:18.434
Micron.

66
00:04:18.384 --> 00:04:22.894
The DRAM and HBM cycle is in its third year of expansion.

67
00:04:22.894 --> 00:04:26.509
Institutional positioning is heavy in the long book.

68
00:04:26.509 --> 00:04:29.916
But the short side has been building since August.

69
00:04:29.916 --> 00:04:33.825
Key support sits at the fifty-two week moving average.

70
00:04:33.825 --> 00:04:38.200
A break below that level triggers the next tranche of stop losses.

71
00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:41.680
The catalyst to watch is the October earnings print.

72
00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:46.864
If the HBM pricing curve flattens, the multiple compresses fast.

73
00:04:46.864 --> 00:04:51.558
In a split Congress scenario, the chip subsidy tailwind holds.

74
00:04:51.558 --> 00:04:56.227
In a Democratic sweep, the antitrust risk premium expands.

75
00:04:56.227 --> 00:04:58.960
The stock underperforms the sector.

76
00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:01.288
Risk management boundary.

77
00:05:01.288 --> 00:05:06.472
If the stock loses the round number support on volume, that is your exit signal.

78
00:05:06.472 --> 00:05:08.310
Exxon Mobil.

79
00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:11.313
This is the oil hedge in the portfolio.

80
00:05:11.313 --> 00:05:19.413
With Chevron's CEO saying there is no path to rapid price normalization, Exxon is the purest play on sustained high oil.

81
00:05:19.413 --> 00:05:24.156
The split Congress scenario protects it from energy policy restrictions.

82
00:05:24.156 --> 00:05:29.830
The Democratic sweep scenario introduces capex penalties and ESG enforcement.

83
00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:32.183
Technicals are constructive.

84
00:05:32.183 --> 00:05:36.239
The stock is holding above its two hundred day moving average.

85
00:05:36.239 --> 00:05:39.487
The key resistance is the prior cycle high.

86
00:05:39.487 --> 00:05:43.286
A breakout on above-average volume confirms the trend.

87
00:05:43.286 --> 00:05:46.693
The risk is a sudden OPEC supply increase.

88
00:05:46.693 --> 00:05:51.558
Or a geopolitical de-escalation that dumps oil ten percent in a week.

89
00:05:51.558 --> 00:05:53.396
Lockheed Martin.

90
00:05:53.396 --> 00:05:58.703
Defense spending is the one budget line that survives any congressional configuration.

91
00:05:58.703 --> 00:06:01.264
Both parties fund the military.

92
00:06:01.264 --> 00:06:06.815
The split Congress scenario means continued order flow with zero political disruption.

93
00:06:06.815 --> 00:06:13.335
The Democratic sweep scenario adds investigation risk if any contracts are tied to the current administration.

94
00:06:13.335 --> 00:06:16.362
The stock trades on a premium multiple.

95
00:06:16.362 --> 00:06:19.928
So any headline-driven de-risking hits it harder.

96
00:06:19.928 --> 00:06:23.776
Watch the defense budget appropriation vote in the House.

97
00:06:23.776 --> 00:06:26.399
That is the near-term catalyst.

98
00:06:26.399 --> 00:06:30.553
Support is the ten percent retracement level from the summer high.

99
00:06:30.553 --> 00:06:33.163
The bottom line for your positioning.

100
00:06:33.163 --> 00:06:35.982
The pre-election window is a grind.

101
00:06:35.982 --> 00:06:37.918
Expect whipsaw.

102
00:06:37.918 --> 00:06:42.967
Expect headline-driven ten percent intraday moves on oil or a court ruling.

103
00:06:42.967 --> 00:06:47.869
The Supreme Court just killed the DOJ mail-in ballot verification rule.

104
00:06:47.869 --> 00:06:52.281
That means November third could produce weeks of counting disputes.

105
00:06:52.281 --> 00:06:54.646
Volatility will spike.

106
00:06:54.646 --> 00:06:57.024
Risk assets sell off.

107
00:06:57.024 --> 00:07:01.901
That is the tail risk every institutional options trader is hedging right now.

108
00:07:01.901 --> 00:07:06.546
But the post-election snapback is the highest-probability trade of the year.

109
00:07:06.546 --> 00:07:08.788
Nineteen for nineteen.

110
00:07:08.788 --> 00:07:12.894
The money is made in the three to six months after the dust settles.

111
00:07:12.894 --> 00:07:15.296
Position for the volatility.

112
00:07:15.296 --> 00:07:17.416
Protect the downside.

113
00:07:17.416 --> 00:07:20.933
And be loaded and ready when the uncertainty resolves.

114
00:07:20.933 --> 00:07:24.940
That concludes today's XiaoFang Finance Morning Brief.

115
00:07:24.940 --> 00:07:29.107
Thank you for listening, trade safely, and see you tomorrow.
